{
  "_doc": "The riding habit's layer stack, under to over, and the ease each layer needs over the one beneath it. One file for the whole outfit. The 'layers' and 'fabrics' blocks are inputs, written by hand from each garment's own code. The 'derived' block is computed from the measured body in the pose strip's extreme poses by `blender/shared/ease.py` (run it through the render queue, or `python3 tools/render-queue/rq_run.py -- blender/base/rq_views.py --figure outfit --mannequin --derive-ease`), never typed in. Girths and ease are in cm; `radial_w` is the same ease as a stand-off from the layer beneath in units of w (the neck's half-width, the vest and bodice drafts' unit), which is what a shell-offset draft such as redingote/bodice.py's RATIOS['ease'] is. Lengths in the code are scene units; CM_PER_UNIT converts.",
  "cm_per_unit": 2.619,
  "cm_per_unit_source": "redingote/pattern.py CM = 165 / 63 (the habit's pieces already measure in this)",
  "poses": [
    "rest",
    "arms-up",
    "arms-fwd",
    "elbows",
    "step",
    "crouch",
    "twist"
  ],
  "regions": {
    "_doc": "Where ease is measured. 'girth' regions are a tape round a band of skin (the convex hull of the band, as a tape bridges hollows); 'width' is the back's width between the arms (the back half of the chest band's hull); 'pair' is a tape round both legs together (what a skirt's or petticoat's hem has to pass in a stride). z is the band's centre in the base figure's landmarks; part says which skin the band is taken from.",
